WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.120
Good Morning John.

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:06.780
Okay, I found out something, and I can’t shut up
about it so you’re gonna have to deal with me.

00:00:06.780 --> 00:00:10.780
A desert island is not a desert island.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:17.480
By which I mean, a desert island can be lush and
covered with water, with big rainforest trees.

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.400
It is not an island that is a desert.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:23.320
It is desert…

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:29.500
an adjective that I have been using my
WHOLE LIFE without knowing that it exists!

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:32.680
“Desert” means uninhabited or deserted.

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:39.180
This word, D-E-S-E-R-T, has four different
definitions and two different pronunciations.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:42.520
One, desert: noun, a place without much water.

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.960
Desert, two: adjective, uninhabited.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:50.520
Desert: verb, where you abandon something,
like a you’re a deserter.

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:55.200
And four, desert: noun, the thing you deserve.

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.380
Another word I’ve been using my whole life
without knowing IT EXISTS!

00:00:59.380 --> 00:01:07.280
When I say, “You got your just deserts,”
I don’t mean, “You got your tasty after meal treat.”

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:14.860
In this case, “just” means like you’re fair,
and “deserts” means “the things you deserve.”

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:23.000
So the “just” part of this idiom is completely irrelevant
because every desert is something that is just,

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:25.800
it is what you deserve definitionally!

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:31.160
And all of this is to say nothing of “dessert”
with the extra S, the delicious thing

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:33.740
that you eat after you’re done with dinner.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:39.520
This led me into a place that was terrible,
and you won’t find out why until the very end.

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:43.500
But of course, it being English, we have words
for all of these things.

00:01:43.500 --> 00:01:49.440
We have words for words that sound or look
like each other but don’t mean the same thing.

00:01:49.440 --> 00:01:52.480
So there are three things here that words
might have in common.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.360
There’s the meaning, there’s how they
sound, and there’s how they’re spelled.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.320
Now in this situation we’re talking about
words that all have different meaning,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.020
so I’m just gonna discard that for now.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.140
And talk about the suffixes that we use for
words that have different meaning but are

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:05.320
pronounced the same or are written the same.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:10.080
When we’re talking about pronunciation, we’re talking
about how they sound, so we use the suffix -phone.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:14.500
And when we’re talking about how they look,
we’re talking about writing, so we use the suffix -graph.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:19.100
And then we have our prefixes:
we have hetero- for different and homo- for same.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.300
So a homophone is something that sounds the
same but has a different definition,

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:25.460
and a homograph is something that looks the
same but has a different definition.

00:02:25.460 --> 00:02:28.920
And then you have the word that we talk about,
which is “homonym,”

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:31.360
and that’s when BOTH of those things are true.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:32.880
So far this makes sense.

00:02:32.880 --> 00:02:35.380
Not super easy to get your head around,
but it makes sense.

00:02:35.380 --> 00:02:38.400
Homonym: same pronunciation, same spelling,
but different meaning

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:39.880
like “desert” (sand)
and “desert” (abandoned).

00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:44.060
And all homonyms are also
homophones and homographs.

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:48.320
But some words are only homophones,
and some words are only homographs.

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:52.220
Like “desert” (to abandon) and “dessert” (sweet).
They sound the same, but they’re spelled different.

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:57.640
And this means that they are also something
called a heterograph, meaning they look different.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:02.980
And then, my friends, there are things that
are spelled the same but sound different.

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:05.800
Homographs, like “desert” (sand)
and “desert” (to abandon).

00:03:05.800 --> 00:03:07.960
These things sound different.

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:09.780
They are heterophones.

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.880
And they are called heteronyms.

00:03:12.880 --> 00:03:14.400
[SEETHING]

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:17.840
This took me a HALF AN HOUR OF MY LIFE
to figure out!

00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:20.080
WHY AREN’T THEY CALLED HETEROPHONES?

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:22.920
John, I’ll see you on Tuesday.

